{
  "teamId": 138,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 St. Louis Cardinals entered the season with high hopes after acquiring Paul Goldschmidt in the offseason, but the early returns were rocky. A blazing April (18-7) gave way to a brutal May collapse (9-18) that threatened to derail the whole campaign before summer even arrived. A costly blown save by Jordan Hicks in Texas in mid-May captured the frustration of a team that couldn't hold leads, and the bullpen — Andrew Miller in particular — proved to be an ongoing source of heartbreak throughout the year.\n\nJune was the month the Cardinals began to find their identity. Dexter Fowler, maligned for years after a massive contract, delivered a three-run walk-off blast against the Mets on June 14th that embodied the team's fighting spirit. Paul Goldschmidt and rookie Tommy Edman emerged as catalysts, with Edman hitting his first career homer in late June and following it up with a grand slam in July. The offense was coming alive, and despite the bullpen's volatility — Miller continued to cough up runs at critical moments — the Cardinals posted a combined 30-22 record in June and July, clawing their way back into contention.\n\nAugust was the month St. Louis seized control of the NL Central race. An 18-9 mark, powered by Lane Thomas's stunning grand slam on August 11th after a devastating blown save the week prior, announced that this team had genuine postseason mettle. Goldschmidt hit his 33rd homer in a wild late-September series against the Diamondbacks, even as John Gant and the staff had trouble locking things down. A late-season 16-12 September record was enough to clinch the division and send the Redbirds to October for the first time since 2015.\n\nIn the NLDS against Atlanta, the Cardinals' story took a dramatic turn. Marcell Ozuna's two-run double in Game 1 staked them to an early lead, and Yadier Molina delivered in his trademark clutch fashion in Game 3. But Carlos Martinez was shelled in Game 2, surrendering doubles to Josh Donaldson and Dansby Swanson in a punishing inning that swung momentum Atlanta's way. The Cardinals won the series but were ultimately stopped in the NLCS, a fitting if bittersweet end to a season defined by resilience, unexpected heroes, and the unrelenting roller coaster of Andrew Miller's bullpen appearances.",
  "chapters": [
